{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler navigating complex political landscapes in the Levant. Raymond inherited the county from his father Raymond II a prominent Crusader knight and military leader. His reign was characterized by both military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering aimed at securing Tripoli's independence and prosperity.\n\n*Early Life and Accession to the County*\n\nRaymond III's early life was steeped in military training and political intrigue. He participated in Crusades alongside his father gaining valuable experience in warfare. Upon his father's death in 1154 Raymond ascended to the County of Tripoli facing immediate challenges from rival powers including the Zengid dynasty of Syria and other Crusader states. He quickly established himself as a capable ruler consolidating his authority through strategic alliances and military strength. He faced constant pressure from Saladin and other Muslim rulers seeking to reclaim lost territories.\n\nRaymond III’s rule involved considerable military activity. He engaged in numerous campaigns against Muslim forces defending Tripoli's borders and expanding its influence. He allied with other Crusader rulers like Bohemond of Antioch and Baldwin of Jerusalem to form a united front against their common enemies. These alliances were often fragile reflecting the shifting power dynamics in the region. His military successes helped maintain Tripoli's autonomy and economic vitality. He also focused on strengthening Tripoli’s defenses building fortifications and improving its infrastructure.\n\n*Diplomacy and Political Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a skilled diplomat adept at navigating the treacherous political landscape of the Levant. He forged alliances with various powers including the Byzantine Empire and the Papacy seeking support against Muslim aggression. He understood the importance of maintaining good relations with both Christian and Muslim rulers even when they were adversaries. He used diplomacy to secure trade agreements and protect Tripoli's commercial interests. This careful balancing act allowed Tripoli to thrive economically despite the constant threat of war. His diplomatic efforts extended to negotiating treaties and establishing peaceful relations with neighboring states when possible.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III's reign marked a period of relative stability and prosperity for the County of Tripoli. He successfully defended the county against numerous attacks and maintained its independence for several decades. His leadership solidified Tripoli's position as a major power in the Levant. He left behind a well-defended county with a strong economy and a reputation for diplomatic skill. Raymond III died in 1199 leaving his son Raymond IV to inherit the county. He is remembered as a capable warrior astute diplomat and effective ruler who played a vital role in the Crusades and the history of the Levant. His legacy continues to be studied by historians interested in the complexities of medieval warfare and political alliances.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.29277363419532776}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.39782649278640747}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.11289401352405548}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0002256377338198945}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5510462364763953e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.867782187531702e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -8.2608